{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want to skip to the implementation? Check out these examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-table-controller","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic TableController"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-app-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic App Table"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-external-atoms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic External Atoms"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-external-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic External State"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"table-state-lit-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State (Lit) Guide"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you boil TanStack Table down to one sentence: TanStack Table is a large state-management coordinator for table states."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Understanding this guide is fundamental to understanding how TanStack Table works and how to interact with it for the best results."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"do-you-need-to-manage-external-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do you need to Manage External State?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You usually do NOT need to manage table state yourself. If you pass nothing to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":", or any of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" table options, TanStack Table will manage its own state internally."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There will be situations where you need to customize how you interact with the internal table state, or even hoist it up to your own scopes. TanStack Table lets you read, subscribe to, or own the state slices that matter to your app. This guide explains how table state works in Lit, how to read it, and when to use external atoms or external state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"state-in-v9","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State in v9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table v9 overhauled state management around TanStack Store. TanStack Store uses the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alien-signals"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation and supports performant derived state. For Lit, the table adapter wires TanStack Store atoms into a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableController"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A table instance has a few state surfaces:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.baseAtoms"},{"type":"text","value":" are the internal writable atoms created from the resolved initial state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" are readonly derived atoms exposed per registered state slice."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"},{"type":"text","value":" is a readonly flat TanStack Store derived by putting all of the registered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" together."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":" is Lit-only selected state. It is the value returned from the selector passed as the second argument to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableController.table(...)"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Lit adapter provides "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"litReactivity()"},{"type":"text","value":" to the table's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"coreReactivityFeature"},{"type":"text","value":". Readonly and writable atoms are TanStack Store atoms. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableController"},{"type":"text","value":" subscribes to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.optionsStore"},{"type":"text","value":"; atom or options changes flowing through those stores call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.requestUpdate()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"feature-based-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature-based State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State slices are only created for the features that are registered in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":". This keeps TanStack Table tree-shakeable and gives TypeScript more accurate state inference."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst table = this.tableController.table({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data: this._data,\n})\n\ntable.atoms.pagination.get()\ntable.atoms.sorting.get()\n\n// table.atoms.rowSelection // TypeScript error unless rowSelectionFeature is registered","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" does not include a feature, its state should not be available in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store.state"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"accessing-table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessing Table State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two different questions when reading table state:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do you only need the current value?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Or should the Lit host update when that value changes?"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a direct atom or store read for the current value. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" in render output when the host should reflect selected table state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"reading-state-without-subscribing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reading State Without Subscribing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The simplest and most performant way to read a current state value is to read the matching atom:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const pagination = table.atoms.pagination.get()\nconst sorting = table.atoms.sorting.get()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also read the current flat store snapshot:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const tableState = table.store.state\nconst pagination = table.store.state.pagination","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These reads are current-value reads. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableController"},{"type":"text","value":" handles host invalidation through its subscriptions to the table store and options store. If the UI needs to stay reactive to table state changes, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":", or a TanStack Store subscription."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"reading-reactive-state-with-tablecontroller","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reading Reactive State with TableController"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second argument to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableController.table(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" is a TanStack Store selector. The selected value is exposed as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":". The default selector selects all registered table state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = this.tableController.table(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: this._data,\n  },\n  (state) => ({\n    pagination: state.pagination,\n  }),\n)\n\ntable.state.pagination","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"selecting-state-with-table-subscribe","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Selecting State with table.subscribe"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" in templates to select a slice of table state while rendering."}]},{"type":"code","value":"private paginationSelector = (state) => ({\n  pagination: state.pagination,\n})\n\n${table.subscribe(\n  table.store,\n  this.paginationSelector,\n  ({ pagination }) => html`\n    <span>Page ${pagination.pageIndex + 1}</span>\n  `,\n)}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The template will only be evaluated when the selected state slice changes. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" API is useful for fine-grained reactivity in templates.\nIt is important to provide a stable reference for the selector function to avoid unnecessary re-renders. You can define the selector as a class property or method to ensure it remains stable across renders."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"setting-table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Setting Table State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You should almost never need to set table state directly. TanStack Table features expose dedicated APIs for interacting with their state, and those APIs are the safest way to make changes."}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.nextPage()\ntable.previousPage()\ntable.setPageIndex(0)\ntable.setPageSize(25)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use APIs like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting(...)"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnFilters(...)"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.toggleVisibility()"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.toggleSelected()"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of manually editing the underlying state object."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you only care about setting starting values, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". If you want to reset a state slice back to its initial value, use that feature's reset API."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you really do need to write a state slice directly, the low-level write surface for internally owned state is the matching base atom:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.baseAtoms.pagination.set((old) => ({\n  ...old,\n  pageIndex: 0,\n}))","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct base atom writes should be rare. If a slice is owned by an external atom passed through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":", write to that external atom instead; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" will read from the external atom, not the internal base atom."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"custom-initial-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Initial State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you only need to customize the starting value for some table state, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". You still do not need to manage that state yourself."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" only applies to registered state slices. It is used to create the table's initial state and is also used by reset APIs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetSorting()"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetPagination()"},{"type":"text","value":". Changing the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" object later does not reset table state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = this.tableController.table({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data: this._data,\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [\n      {\n        id: 'age',\n        desc: true,\n      },\n    ],\n    pagination: {\n      pageIndex: 0,\n      pageSize: 25,\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not provide the same state slice in multiple ownership places unless you intentionally want one to win. For a slice like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pagination"},{"type":"text","value":", prefer exactly one of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" as the source of truth. External atoms take precedence over external "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":"; external "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" syncs into the table's internal base atom."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"resetting-to-initial-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resetting to Initial State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature reset APIs reset to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" by default. Many reset APIs also accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" to reset to that feature's blank/default state instead:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.resetSorting()\ntable.resetPagination()\ntable.resetPagination(true)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Slice reset APIs like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resetPagination()"},{"type":"text","value":" update through that feature's state updater and can update an externally owned atom. The core "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" API resets the internal base atoms, so do not use it as the primary way to reset state that is owned by external atoms."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"controlled-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need easy access to table state in other parts of your application, you can control individual state slices. In v9, external atoms are the recommended way to do this when you want atomic ownership."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"external-atoms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External Atoms"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use external atoms when the app should own one or more table state slices. Create stable writable atoms with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAtom"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/store"},{"type":"text","value":", pass them to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":", and read them where needed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\nimport {\n  TableController,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  type PaginationState,\n} from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n})\n\nconst paginationAtom = createAtom<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = this.tableController.table({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data: this._data,\n  atoms: {\n    pagination: paginationAtom,\n  },\n  manualPagination: true,\n})\n\nconst pagination = paginationAtom.get()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option for a slice, you do not need to add the matching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" option."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"external-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The classic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported. This can be convenient for simple Lit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@state()"},{"type":"text","value":" integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less atomic than external atoms."}]},{"type":"code","value":"@state()\nprivate _sorting: SortingState = []\n\nprotected render() {\n  const table = this.tableController.table({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: this._data,\n    state: {\n      sorting: this._sorting,\n    },\n    onSortingChange: (updater) => {\n      this._sorting =\n        updater instanceof Function ? updater(this._sorting) : updater\n    },\n  })\n\n  return html`...`\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStateChange"},{"type":"text","value":" option is no longer part of the v9 "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableController"},{"type":"text","value":" state model. v9 encourages keeping table state slices atomic and separated for performance."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"on-state-change-callbacks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On State Change Callbacks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks are useful when you are controlling a matching slice through the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" option. They receive either a raw value or an updater function."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you provide an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" callback, also provide the corresponding value in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":". For example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSortingChange"},{"type":"text","value":" should be paired with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"onPaginationChange: (updater) => {\n  this._pagination =\n    updater instanceof Function ? updater(this._pagination) : updater\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"state-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most complex states in TanStack Table have their own TypeScript types that you can import and use."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  TableController,\n  type PaginationState,\n  type RowSelectionState,\n  type SortingState,\n  type TableState,\n} from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\n@state()\nprivate _sorting: SortingState = [\n  {\n    id: 'age',\n    desc: true,\n  },\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableState<typeof features>"},{"type":"text","value":" is inferred from the features registered on that table:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"type MyTableState = TableState<typeof features>","lang":"ts"}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"table-state-lit-guide","text":"Table State (Lit) Guide","level":2},{"id":"do-you-need-to-manage-external-state","text":"Do you need to Manage External State?","level":3},{"id":"state-in-v9","text":"State in v9","level":3},{"id":"feature-based-state","text":"Feature-based State","level":3},{"id":"accessing-table-state","text":"Accessing Table State","level":3},{"id":"reading-state-without-subscribing","text":"Reading State Without Subscribing","level":4},{"id":"reading-reactive-state-with-tablecontroller","text":"Reading Reactive State with TableController","level":4},{"id":"selecting-state-with-table-subscribe","text":"Selecting State with table.subscribe","level":4},{"id":"setting-table-state","text":"Setting Table State","level":3},{"id":"custom-initial-state","text":"Custom Initial State","level":3},{"id":"resetting-to-initial-state","text":"Resetting to Initial State","level":4},{"id":"controlled-state","text":"Controlled State","level":3},{"id":"external-atoms","text":"External Atoms","level":4},{"id":"external-state","text":"External State","level":4},{"id":"on-state-change-callbacks","text":"On State Change Callbacks","level":5},{"id":"state-types","text":"State Types","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these examples:\n\n- [Basic TableController](../examples/basic-table-controller)\n- [Basic App Table](../examples/basic-app-table)\n- [Basic External Atoms](../examples/basic-external-atoms)\n- [Basic External State](../examples/basic-external-state)\n\n## Table State (Lit) Guide\n\n> **If you boil TanStack Table down to one sentence: TanStack Table is a large state-management coordinator for table states.**\n\nUnderstanding this guide is fundamental to understanding how TanStack Table works and how to interact with it for the best results.\n\n### Do you need to Manage External State?\n\nYou usually do NOT need to manage table state yourself. If you pass nothing to `initialState`, `atoms`, `state`, or any of the `on[State]Change` table options, TanStack Table will manage its own state internally.\n\nThere will be situations where you need to customize how you interact with the internal table state, or even hoist it up to your own scopes. TanStack Table lets you read, subscribe to, or own the state slices that matter to your app. This guide explains how table state works in Lit, how to read it, and when to use external atoms or external state.\n\n### State in v9\n\nTanStack Table v9 overhauled state management around TanStack Store. TanStack Store uses the `alien-signals` implementation and supports performant derived state. For Lit, the table adapter wires TanStack Store atoms into a `TableController`.\n\nA table instance has a few state surfaces:\n\n- `table.baseAtoms` are the internal writable atoms created from the resolved initial state.\n- `table.atoms` are readonly derived atoms exposed per registered state slice.\n- `table.store` is a readonly flat TanStack Store derived by putting all of the registered `table.atoms` together.\n- `table.state` is Lit-only selected state. It is the value returned from the selector passed as the second argument to `tableController.table(...)`.\n\nThe Lit adapter provides `litReactivity()` to the table's `coreReactivityFeature`. Readonly and writable atoms are TanStack Store atoms. `TableController` subscribes to `table.store` and `table.optionsStore`; atom or options changes flowing through those stores call `host.requestUpdate()`.\n\n### Feature-based State\n\nState slices are only created for the features that are registered in `features`. This keeps TanStack Table tree-shakeable and gives TypeScript more accurate state inference.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst table = this.tableController.table({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data: this._data,\n})\n\ntable.atoms.pagination.get()\ntable.atoms.sorting.get()\n\n// table.atoms.rowSelection // TypeScript error unless rowSelectionFeature is registered\n```\n\nIf `features` does not include a feature, its state should not be available in `table.atoms`, `table.store.state`, `table.state`, `initialState`, `state`, or `atoms`.\n\n### Accessing Table State\n\nThere are two different questions when reading table state:\n\n- Do you only need the current value?\n- Or should the Lit host update when that value changes?\n\nUse a direct atom or store read for the current value. Use `table.state` or `table.subscribe` in render output when the host should reflect selected table state.\n\n#### Reading State Without Subscribing\n\nThe simplest and most performant way to read a current state value is to read the matching atom:\n\n```ts\nconst pagination = table.atoms.pagination.get()\nconst sorting = table.atoms.sorting.get()\n```\n\nYou can also read the current flat store snapshot:\n\n```ts\nconst tableState = table.store.state\nconst pagination = table.store.state.pagination\n```\n\nThese reads are current-value reads. The `TableController` handles host invalidation through its subscriptions to the table store and options store. If the UI needs to stay reactive to table state changes, use `table.state`, `table.subscribe`, or a TanStack Store subscription.\n\n#### Reading Reactive State with TableController\n\nThe second argument to `tableController.table(...)` is a TanStack Store selector. The selected value is exposed as `table.state`. The default selector selects all registered table state.\n\n```ts\nconst table = this.tableController.table(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: this._data,\n  },\n  (state) => ({\n    pagination: state.pagination,\n  }),\n)\n\ntable.state.pagination\n```\n\n#### Selecting State with table.subscribe\n\nUse `table.subscribe` in templates to select a slice of table state while rendering.\n\n```ts\nprivate paginationSelector = (state) => ({\n  pagination: state.pagination,\n})\n\n${table.subscribe(\n  table.store,\n  this.paginationSelector,\n  ({ pagination }) => html`\n    <span>Page ${pagination.pageIndex + 1}</span>\n  `,\n)}\n```\n\nThe template will only be evaluated when the selected state slice changes. The `table.subscribe` API is useful for fine-grained reactivity in templates.\nIt is important to provide a stable reference for the selector function to avoid unnecessary re-renders. You can define the selector as a class property or method to ensure it remains stable across renders.\n\n### Setting Table State\n\nYou should almost never need to set table state directly. TanStack Table features expose dedicated APIs for interacting with their state, and those APIs are the safest way to make changes.\n\n```ts\ntable.nextPage()\ntable.previousPage()\ntable.setPageIndex(0)\ntable.setPageSize(25)\n```\n\nUse APIs like `table.setSorting(...)`, `table.setColumnFilters(...)`, `column.toggleVisibility()`, or `row.toggleSelected()` instead of manually editing the underlying state object.\n\nIf you only care about setting starting values, use `initialState`. If you want to reset a state slice back to its initial value, use that feature's reset API.\n\nIf you really do need to write a state slice directly, the low-level write surface for internally owned state is the matching base atom:\n\n```ts\ntable.baseAtoms.pagination.set((old) => ({\n  ...old,\n  pageIndex: 0,\n}))\n```\n\nDirect base atom writes should be rare. If a slice is owned by an external atom passed through `atoms`, write to that external atom instead; `table.atoms.pagination` will read from the external atom, not the internal base atom.\n\n### Custom Initial State\n\nIf you only need to customize the starting value for some table state, use `initialState`. You still do not need to manage that state yourself.\n\n`initialState` only applies to registered state slices. It is used to create the table's initial state and is also used by reset APIs such as `table.resetSorting()` or `table.resetPagination()`. Changing the `initialState` object later does not reset table state.\n\n```ts\nconst table = this.tableController.table({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data: this._data,\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [\n      {\n        id: 'age',\n        desc: true,\n      },\n    ],\n    pagination: {\n      pageIndex: 0,\n      pageSize: 25,\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do not provide the same state slice in multiple ownership places unless you intentionally want one to win. For a slice like `pagination`, prefer exactly one of `initialState.pagination`, `atoms.pagination`, or `state.pagination` as the source of truth. External atoms take precedence over external `state`; external `state` syncs into the table's internal base atom.\n\n#### Resetting to Initial State\n\nFeature reset APIs reset to `table.initialState` by default. Many reset APIs also accept `true` to reset to that feature's blank/default state instead:\n\n```ts\ntable.resetSorting()\ntable.resetPagination()\ntable.resetPagination(true)\n```\n\nSlice reset APIs like `resetPagination()` update through that feature's state updater and can update an externally owned atom. The core `table.reset()` API resets the internal base atoms, so do not use it as the primary way to reset state that is owned by external atoms.\n\n### Controlled State\n\nIf you need easy access to table state in other parts of your application, you can control individual state slices. In v9, external atoms are the recommended way to do this when you want atomic ownership.\n\n#### External Atoms\n\nUse external atoms when the app should own one or more table state slices. Create stable writable atoms with `createAtom` from `@tanstack/store`, pass them to `atoms`, and read them where needed.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\nimport {\n  TableController,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  type PaginationState,\n} from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n})\n\nconst paginationAtom = createAtom<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = this.tableController.table({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data: this._data,\n  atoms: {\n    pagination: paginationAtom,\n  },\n  manualPagination: true,\n})\n\nconst pagination = paginationAtom.get()\n```\n\nWhen using the `atoms` option for a slice, you do not need to add the matching `on[State]Change` option.\n\n#### External State\n\nThe classic `state` plus `on[State]Change` pattern is still supported. This can be convenient for simple Lit `@state()` integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less atomic than external atoms.\n\n```ts\n@state()\nprivate _sorting: SortingState = []\n\nprotected render() {\n  const table = this.tableController.table({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: this._data,\n    state: {\n      sorting: this._sorting,\n    },\n    onSortingChange: (updater) => {\n      this._sorting =\n        updater instanceof Function ? updater(this._sorting) : updater\n    },\n  })\n\n  return html`...`\n}\n```\n\nThe v8-style `onStateChange` option is no longer part of the v9 `TableController` state model. v9 encourages keeping table state slices atomic and separated for performance.\n\n##### On State Change Callbacks\n\nThe `on[State]Change` callbacks are useful when you are controlling a matching slice through the `state` option. They receive either a raw value or an updater function.\n\nIf you provide an `on[State]Change` callback, also provide the corresponding value in `state`. For example, `onSortingChange` should be paired with `state.sorting`.\n\n```ts\nonPaginationChange: (updater) => {\n  this._pagination =\n    updater instanceof Function ? updater(this._pagination) : updater\n}\n```\n\n### State Types\n\nMost complex states in TanStack Table have their own TypeScript types that you can import and use.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  TableController,\n  type PaginationState,\n  type RowSelectionState,\n  type SortingState,\n  type TableState,\n} from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\n@state()\nprivate _sorting: SortingState = [\n  {\n    id: 'age',\n    desc: true,\n  },\n]\n```\n\n`TableState<typeof features>` is inferred from the features registered on that table:\n\n```ts\ntype MyTableState = TableState<typeof features>\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Table State (Lit) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these examples:\n\n- [Basic TableController](../examples/basic-table-controller)\n- [Basic App Table](../examples/basic-app-table)\n- [Basic External Atoms](../examples/basic-external-atoms)\n- [Basic External State](../examples/basic-external-state)\n\n## Table State (Lit) Guide\n\n> **If you boil TanStack Table down to one sentence: TanStack Table is a large state-management coordinator for table states.**\n\nUnderstanding this guide is fundamental to understanding how TanStack Table works and how to interact with it for the best results.\n\n### Do you need to Manage External State?\n\nYou usually do NOT need to manage table state yourself. If you pass nothing to `initialState`, `atoms`, `state`, or any of the `on[State]Change` table options, TanStack Table will manage its own state internally.\n\nThere will be situations where you need to customize how you interact with the internal table state, or even hoist it up to your own scopes. TanStack Table lets you read, subscribe to, or own the state slices that matter to your app. This guide explains how table state works in Lit, how to read it, and when to use external atoms or external state.\n\n### State in v9\n\nTanStack Table v9 overhauled state management around TanStack Store. TanStack Store uses the `alien-signals` implementation and supports performant derived state. For Lit, the table adapter wires TanStack Store atoms into a `TableController`.\n\nA table instance has a few state surfaces:\n\n- `table.baseAtoms` are the internal writable atoms created from the resolved initial state.\n- `table.atoms` are readonly derived atoms exposed per registered state slice.\n- `table.store` is a readonly flat TanStack Store derived by putting all of the registered `table.atoms` together.\n- `table.state` is Lit-only selected state. It is the value returned from the selector passed as the second argument to `tableController.table(...)`.\n\nThe Lit adapter provides `litReactivity()` to the table's `coreReactivityFeature`. Readonly and writable atoms are TanStack Store atoms. `TableController` subscribes to `table.store` and `table.optionsStore`; atom or options changes flowing through those stores call `host.requestUpdate()`.\n\n### Feature-based State\n\nState slices are only created for the features that are registered in `features`. This keeps TanStack Table tree-shakeable and gives TypeScript more accurate state inference.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst table = this.tableController.table({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data: this._data,\n})\n\ntable.atoms.pagination.get()\ntable.atoms.sorting.get()\n\n// table.atoms.rowSelection // TypeScript error unless rowSelectionFeature is registered\n```\n\nIf `features` does not include a feature, its state should not be available in `table.atoms`, `table.store.state`, `table.state`, `initialState`, `state`, or `atoms`.\n\n### Accessing Table State\n\nThere are two different questions when reading table state:\n\n- Do you only need the current value?\n- Or should the Lit host update when that value changes?\n\nUse a direct atom or store read for the current value. Use `table.state` or `table.subscribe` in render output when the host should reflect selected table state.\n\n#### Reading State Without Subscribing\n\nThe simplest and most performant way to read a current state value is to read the matching atom:\n\n```ts\nconst pagination = table.atoms.pagination.get()\nconst sorting = table.atoms.sorting.get()\n```\n\nYou can also read the current flat store snapshot:\n\n```ts\nconst tableState = table.store.state\nconst pagination = table.store.state.pagination\n```\n\nThese reads are current-value reads. The `TableController` handles host invalidation through its subscriptions to the table store and options store. If the UI needs to stay reactive to table state changes, use `table.state`, `table.subscribe`, or a TanStack Store subscription.\n\n#### Reading Reactive State with TableController\n\nThe second argument to `tableController.table(...)` is a TanStack Store selector. The selected value is exposed as `table.state`. The default selector selects all registered table state.\n\n```ts\nconst table = this.tableController.table(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: this._data,\n  },\n  (state) => ({\n    pagination: state.pagination,\n  }),\n)\n\ntable.state.pagination\n```\n\n#### Selecting State with table.subscribe\n\nUse `table.subscribe` in templates to select a slice of table state while rendering.\n\n```ts\nprivate paginationSelector = (state) => ({\n  pagination: state.pagination,\n})\n\n${table.subscribe(\n  table.store,\n  this.paginationSelector,\n  ({ pagination }) => html`\n    <span>Page ${pagination.pageIndex + 1}</span>\n  `,\n)}\n```\n\nThe template will only be evaluated when the selected state slice changes. The `table.subscribe` API is useful for fine-grained reactivity in templates.\nIt is important to provide a stable reference for the selector function to avoid unnecessary re-renders. You can define the selector as a class property or method to ensure it remains stable across renders.\n\n### Setting Table State\n\nYou should almost never need to set table state directly. TanStack Table features expose dedicated APIs for interacting with their state, and those APIs are the safest way to make changes.\n\n```ts\ntable.nextPage()\ntable.previousPage()\ntable.setPageIndex(0)\ntable.setPageSize(25)\n```\n\nUse APIs like `table.setSorting(...)`, `table.setColumnFilters(...)`, `column.toggleVisibility()`, or `row.toggleSelected()` instead of manually editing the underlying state object.\n\nIf you only care about setting starting values, use `initialState`. If you want to reset a state slice back to its initial value, use that feature's reset API.\n\nIf you really do need to write a state slice directly, the low-level write surface for internally owned state is the matching base atom:\n\n```ts\ntable.baseAtoms.pagination.set((old) => ({\n  ...old,\n  pageIndex: 0,\n}))\n```\n\nDirect base atom writes should be rare. If a slice is owned by an external atom passed through `atoms`, write to that external atom instead; `table.atoms.pagination` will read from the external atom, not the internal base atom.\n\n### Custom Initial State\n\nIf you only need to customize the starting value for some table state, use `initialState`. You still do not need to manage that state yourself.\n\n`initialState` only applies to registered state slices. It is used to create the table's initial state and is also used by reset APIs such as `table.resetSorting()` or `table.resetPagination()`. Changing the `initialState` object later does not reset table state.\n\n```ts\nconst table = this.tableController.table({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data: this._data,\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [\n      {\n        id: 'age',\n        desc: true,\n      },\n    ],\n    pagination: {\n      pageIndex: 0,\n      pageSize: 25,\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do not provide the same state slice in multiple ownership places unless you intentionally want one to win. For a slice like `pagination`, prefer exactly one of `initialState.pagination`, `atoms.pagination`, or `state.pagination` as the source of truth. External atoms take precedence over external `state`; external `state` syncs into the table's internal base atom.\n\n#### Resetting to Initial State\n\nFeature reset APIs reset to `table.initialState` by default. Many reset APIs also accept `true` to reset to that feature's blank/default state instead:\n\n```ts\ntable.resetSorting()\ntable.resetPagination()\ntable.resetPagination(true)\n```\n\nSlice reset APIs like `resetPagination()` update through that feature's state updater and can update an externally owned atom. The core `table.reset()` API resets the internal base atoms, so do not use it as the primary way to reset state that is owned by external atoms.\n\n### Controlled State\n\nIf you need easy access to table state in other parts of your application, you can control individual state slices. In v9, external atoms are the recommended way to do this when you want atomic ownership.\n\n#### External Atoms\n\nUse external atoms when the app should own one or more table state slices. Create stable writable atoms with `createAtom` from `@tanstack/store`, pass them to `atoms`, and read them where needed.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\nimport {\n  TableController,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  type PaginationState,\n} from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n})\n\nconst paginationAtom = createAtom<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = this.tableController.table({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data: this._data,\n  atoms: {\n    pagination: paginationAtom,\n  },\n  manualPagination: true,\n})\n\nconst pagination = paginationAtom.get()\n```\n\nWhen using the `atoms` option for a slice, you do not need to add the matching `on[State]Change` option.\n\n#### External State\n\nThe classic `state` plus `on[State]Change` pattern is still supported. This can be convenient for simple Lit `@state()` integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less atomic than external atoms.\n\n```ts\n@state()\nprivate _sorting: SortingState = []\n\nprotected render() {\n  const table = this.tableController.table({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: this._data,\n    state: {\n      sorting: this._sorting,\n    },\n    onSortingChange: (updater) => {\n      this._sorting =\n        updater instanceof Function ? updater(this._sorting) : updater\n    },\n  })\n\n  return html`...`\n}\n```\n\nThe v8-style `onStateChange` option is no longer part of the v9 `TableController` state model. v9 encourages keeping table state slices atomic and separated for performance.\n\n##### On State Change Callbacks\n\nThe `on[State]Change` callbacks are useful when you are controlling a matching slice through the `state` option. They receive either a raw value or an updater function.\n\nIf you provide an `on[State]Change` callback, also provide the corresponding value in `state`. For example, `onSortingChange` should be paired with `state.sorting`.\n\n```ts\nonPaginationChange: (updater) => {\n  this._pagination =\n    updater instanceof Function ? updater(this._pagination) : updater\n}\n```\n\n### State Types\n\nMost complex states in TanStack Table have their own TypeScript types that you can import and use.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  TableController,\n  type PaginationState,\n  type RowSelectionState,\n  type SortingState,\n  type TableState,\n} from '@tanstack/lit-table'\n\n@state()\nprivate _sorting: SortingState = [\n  {\n    id: 'age',\n    desc: true,\n  },\n]\n```\n\n`TableState<typeof features>` is inferred from the features registered on that table:\n\n```ts\ntype MyTableState = TableState<typeof features>\n```\n"}
